Z||000006||x-thumpr-x||11-23-2001||09:36 PM||||I'm in the process of removing the whole cradle. Dropping the whole engine and tranny to get a better look what shape it's in. Have it all read to just lift the body and can't get one stinking cradle bolt off!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"><BR>Looks like it got a just a wee bit rusty and the nut now spins with the bolt. So I got to cut if off somehow. Already have a way to fix the problem when I install a new bolt and nut. Just getting the old one out will be the ***** .||||reg||1
Z||000007||MooCow||11-23-2001||10:13 PM||||Hey GTMike I take it you are doing this all by yourself??  I have to just replace my tranny is all.  I plan on doing it the Ed Parks way but I have never done anything like this before.  Any other tips before I did in it again??  Last time I stop right after I got the tire off cause I didn't feel like working on it and it was very cold out.  So far you seem to be doing a good job.<P>Thanks Ken<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/forum_pic.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||GTMike||11-23-2001||10:45 PM||||well i have run out of freaking light bulbs for my shop light. and i can't see   i'm thining of calling it for the night.   i got the clutch on and the trany back on  (only by an act of god)   <P>it was a pain in that a$$  its hard to hold the trany up and line up the pass side drive shaft and get the splines to line up on the input shaft.   <P>im just finishing up the tranny mounts then i'll be putting the susp. back on.    the hard part is over and its all down hill from here.<P>this would be a very hard job for some one who had never done tranny and engie swaps before.   its at harder do change the cluth on a fiero than a rear wheel drive car or truck.<P>it taks alot of jacking up and down, move the jacks  up them put a jack stand then move again.  <P>as for tips....   the drive shafts poped out easy and when putting the pass back in i didn't "set" it untill i was done putting the bolts back in that hold that dust cover on the bottum of the bell housing.  after they are in i took a screw drive and a hammer and taped it back in.<P>also  didn't disconnect any lines for the breaks or he slave.  i could easly tie them out of the way.  i also left most of the brackets on that the shift cables connect to. the only brckets that i took off were the on for the slave and the bracket that is attached to one of the shift cables. <P>well i'm off to find some light bulbs. <P>hey any one know of a guy i ft collins that drives a notch back black v6?   <P>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Back On Holiday||11-25-2001||07:46 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by x-thumpr-x:<BR><B>I'm in the process of removing the whole cradle. Dropping the whole engine and tranny to get a better look what shape it's in. Have it all read to just lift the body and can't get one stinking cradle bolt off!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"><BR>Looks like it got a just a wee bit rusty and the nut now spins with the bolt. So I got to cut if off somehow. Already have a way to fix the problem when I install a new bolt and nut. Just getting the old one out will be the ***** .</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I got this from a guy on the Fiero.org Digest on how to fix that, I used this method...chiseling out a square section and it gives you access to the nut on top.<P>----- Original Message ----- <BR>From: JimAnnaJen@aol.com <BR>To: fiero-digest@fiero.org <BR>Sent: Monday, April 23, 2001 10:19 PM<BR>Subject: Rear cradle bolt solutions-<P>Thanks for all the suggestions on replacing the rear cradle weld nut! <BR>Several list members have said that there is just a sheet metal cover over <BR>the nut, so just chisel it out. <BR>Suggestions: <BR>-Removing the rear bumper to get access to the old nut <BR>-Using epoxy weld material to bond the new bolt to the frame <BR>-Replace the old bolt with a stainless steel one (smart idea) <BR>-Using the existing access hole under the plastic wheel shield on an '88 <BR>Formula <BR>(It sounds like late models recognized this design flaw and made a correction <BR>by adding an access hole - must have been after my '88 was made.) <BR>Since I already cut a 1 inch square hole in the channel, I will be able to <BR>chisel out the sheet metal cover, and remove the old weld nut.  This is my <BR>plan and based on the experience of other list members it will work. <BR>My plan is to install the new bolt through a large washer and into a locknut <BR>(if I can find one).  I'll just use the new hole in the channel to put a <BR>wrench on the nut and just not worry about the possibility of having another <BR>rustwelded bolt.   <BR>Thanks for all the responses. <BR>Jim <BR>'88 4 banger <P>Note: there were responses to the "Stainless steel nut/bolt where it was determined that it prob wouldn't be a good idea based on the fact that alot of weight it on that and it wouldn't prob hold up"<BR>but I wanted to quote the whole msg.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Back On Holiday (edited 11-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||rogergarrison||11-25-2001||10:49 AM||||I was just going to say that clutch is way more than a 'morning' job. Usually more like 8-10 hours   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> (remember the saying 'plan on twice as long as you think' for Fieros)<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 11-25-2001).]||||reg||1
